5	- Storage side (mechanism D): CRC-32, the 48-byte record format every process will use, and an append-only store file that refuses bad records and stops cleanly after a failed write. 116 tests, all passing, sanitizers clean, and valgrind clean on the Pi.
6	- Wrote everything up in the repo so your Claude has the full picture.
7	
8	**To get caught up**
9	1. `git pull`
10	2. Read `docs/progress-log.md`. `CLAUDE.md` points your Claude to it automatically, but it's worth reading yourself too. It has where every milestone stands, what the code guarantees, the record format rules, and the open work.
11	
12	**A piece you could take tonight: the sensor-reading code in `src/sensors/`** (your area on the ownership map). None of it needs the sensors plugged in to start:
13	- **Conversion functions first.** These turn raw register bytes into real units (temperatures, humidity, pressure, light counts). They're plain math with no I/O, so you can write tests for them right now using the worked examples in each datasheet. The MLX90614 is the quickest one to start with, and the BME280 has the most math to get right.
14	- **Then the Linux I2C layer**: open `/dev/i2c-1` and read the registers.
